note bottom
Dependency rule: arrows only point downwards.
L5 has no project imports; L3 never imports L2 or L0;
infrastructure (L4) knows tables but not job logic.
Label prefix is the directory the module lives in:
api/ contracts/ download/ bilibili/ host/ infra/ common/.
Directories group by capability; the arrows answer to the
layer, not the directory (bilibili/bili_http.py is L4 and
infra/events.py is L2). Only one edge crosses a capability
boundary: bilibili/ reuses download/ (output_paths,
subtitle_plan, ytdlp_service, job_read_model).
Inside L0 the direction is fixed: main.py assembles,
api/routers/* are leaves, and the support modules
(api_support, job_artifacts, api_context) never import a router.
Two deliberate separations: parsing the proxy (proxy.py)
is apart from proving it works (connectivity.py), and
producing a cookie report (cookie_health.py) is apart from
importing cookies (browser_cookies.py).
The bilibili side repeats that split: bili_http.py knows no
endpoint, bili_meta.py and bili_policy.py never send a
request, and the "it really published" verdict lives in
bili_upload.py only after bili_api.py reconciles the list.
This figure is checked, not trusted: scripts/check_diagram_edges.py
compares this edge set with the real imports in backend/app
(an edge that no longer exists is an error, not a leftover).
api/routers/* is the only aggregated node; a module that is
not drawn here is reported by that script.
Violations to watch: importing job_manager from
ytdlp_service, or importing yt-dlp types into schemas.
end note
